--Windows PowerShell--

Install Git:
winget install --id Git.Git -e --source winget

Configure Git (First-Time Setup):
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

Verify Configuration:
git config --global --list


-Clone a Repository- (either in Git Bash or PowerShell)

Navigate to Where You Want the Repo:
for example: cd Documents/GitHub

If the Folder Doesn't Exist, Create it:
mkdir -p Documents/GitHub
cd Documents/GitHub

Clone the Repo:
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

MadLibs Repo: git clone https://github.com/EmilyKedenburg/ACM-MadLibs.git

Navigate to Repo:
example: cd ACM-MadLibs

Verify Everything Worked:
git status

You should see something similar to:
On branch master
Your branch is up to date with 'origin/master'.

nothing to commit, working tree clean


-Creating a New Branch-

Check Your Current Branch:
git branch

Example outpit:
* master
  emilyk
  safecopy

The * indicates which branch you're currently on.
Important: Your new branch will be created based on whichever branch you're currently using.

Create Your New Branch:
git switch -c new-branch-name

You should see:
Switched to a new branch 'new-branch-name'

This creates a new branch under the specified name and automatically switches you to that branch.

Push the New Branch to GitHub:
git push -u new-branch-name

The -u sets the upstream tracking branch, meaning future pushes can simply use:
git push


-Pushing Changes-

Stage Your Changes for Commit:
git add .

The . adds all new changes to be uploaded.

Commit Your Changes with a Message:
git commit -m "Your commit message"

Push Your Changes:
git push


-Switching Branches-

See All Local Branch:
git branch

See Local and Remote Branches"
git branch -a

Switch Back to Your Previous Branch:
git switch -

Switch Branch:
git switch branch-name


-Pulling Changes-
git pull